  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nIssued at 300 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\n30-70% chance of rain Friday into Saturday morning:\n\nThe aforementioned surface warm front will shift northeast\ncloser to the area Friday night. At the same time, another mid-\nlevel wave will progress across the central Plains, providing\nsome lift and low-level mass response overtop the surface\nfront. This will pave the way for another shield of showers and\nthunderstorms Friday night into Saturday morning, mainly across\ncentral Missouri (40-70%). Global models within the LREF are\nstill struggling a bit on exact timing and placement of the\nfeatures at play (as evidenced by the low PoPs and QPF values in\nthe current forecast). Taking a look at ESATs, though, this does\nlook like a classic low-level jet heavy rain/training setup. a\n~95th percentile LLJ for this time of year (20-30 kts), is\nforecast to develop as a result of synoptic lift from the mid-\nlevel wave. This will be pumping in Gulf moisture with PWATs\nreaching the 97.5-99th percentile of 2.0-2.1\", leading to a belt\nof stronger than climatologically normal integrated moisture\ntransport into central Missouri. Convergence along the warm\nfront is expected to be oriented NW-SE across the area, which\nwith the northwesterly upper-level pattern, will result in areas\nof training. Therefore, heavy rainfall and potential flooding is\npossible with this system, especially across central Missouri\nwhere WPC has outlined in a SLight (2 of 4) Risk (generally\nalong and north of Highway 60). \n\nIn addition to uncertainties in the system location and timing\nin ensemble members, instability does look to be a tad meager.\nDeterministic medium range models (e.g., NAM), depict values\nless than 500 J/kg north of the warm front where the axis of\nrainfall is forecast to be around. CAM members confirm this for\nthe beginning of the event with simulated reflectivity generally\nshowing spotty showers. That said, there could be some washing\nout of the instability signal with a large precipitation\nfootprint in the lower resolution models. Indeed, the RRFS CAM\ndoes bring more robust convection later Friday night, which\ncould bring the higher rainfall amounts. Either way, with PWATs\nwell in excess of 2 inches, even showers can be efficient\nrainfall producers, and training over any location could lead to\nlocalized flooding. Stay up to date with the forecast for more\nrefinements in rainfall amounts and locations.\n\n\nHeat builds back into the region next week:\n\nFollowing the surface warm front, the pattern will wiggle back\neastward with the upper-level high building back into the\nregion. NBM 25th-75th percentile spreads tighten up and\nincrease with highs reaching the middle 90s to lower 100s Sunday\ninto next week. This is well-above normal with EFI showing\nvalues of 0.8-0.9 (80-90% of models above climatological normal\nrange. \n\nLows will also be quite mild in the middle to upper 70s.\nWith NBM dewpoints forecast to be in the upper 60s to middle \n70s, Heat Index values are forecast to be in the 100-110 range. \n12Z LREF forecasts place a 30-40% chance for Heat Advisory \ncriteria being met (Heat Index > 105 F), and a 10-20% chance of \nExtreme Heat Warning criteria being met (Heat Index > 110 F) in \nsome spots Sunday through Tuesday. This will largely depend on \nthe amount of moisture. Some long range members show corn-sweat-\nlike dewpoints nearing 80 F, while others show the dryline \nbeing close to the area, with dewpoints closer to the 60s. These\nwould have large implications on the upper-end of the Heat \nIndex spectrum.\n\nDuring this period, chances of rain will be low, but not\nnecessarily zero. Northwesterly flow aloft may bring in some\nremnant shower/thunderstorm complexes, and with high moisture in\nthe area, pop-up showers and thunderstorms would also be a\npossibility. For now, confidence is too low to mention anything\nabove 15%.",
